---
import PracticeLayout from '../../layouts/PracticeLayout.astro';
import CrossLinkChip from '../../components/CrossLinkChip.astro';
import WarningCallout from '../../components/WarningCallout.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Data Brokers', href: '/data-brokers' },
  { label: 'Data Broker Industry' },
];
---

<PracticeLayout
  title="Data Broker Industry"
  description="Data brokers compile detailed profiles on individuals from public records, purchase history, and behavioral data — and sell them. Opt-outs exist, but they are piecemeal."
  subtitle="Profiles built without your knowledge, sold without your involvement."
  lastVerified="March 23, 2026"
  platformCount={0}
  severity="critical"
  severityText="Data broker profiles are built from sources most people never interact with directly: court records, property filings, voter registration, loyalty program purchase data, and data purchased from other brokers. There is no central opt-out. Each broker maintains a separate database with a separate opt-out process. Opting out of one does not affect the others."
  category="Data Industry Overview"
  alsoKnownAs="People search sites, consumer data industry, data aggregators"
  platforms={['Acxiom', 'LexisNexis', 'Spokeo', 'WhitePages', 'BeenVerified']}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>

  <!-- WHAT IT IS -->
  <div slot="what-it-is">
    <p>Data brokers are companies that collect personal information about individuals from a variety of sources, aggregate it into profiles, and sell access to those profiles to other businesses. Most people have never interacted with a data broker directly, but most people have a profile at multiple brokers.</p>

    <p>The industry has two major segments. Consumer-facing data brokers operate websites where individuals can look up other people: Spokeo, WhitePages, BeenVerified, Intelius, and similar services. These are sometimes called people-search sites. B2B data brokers sell to businesses rather than individuals: Acxiom, LexisNexis, Experian's data services division, Equifax's Kount and WorkNumber products, and similar companies. The B2B segment is significantly larger and has substantially less public visibility.</p>

    <p>A data broker profile typically includes name, current and historical addresses, phone numbers, email addresses, relatives and household members, estimated income range, property ownership and value, vehicle records, court records (civil and criminal), professional licenses, and inferred interests and behavioral categories. Consumer-facing brokers surface most of this directly. B2B brokers package it into proprietary scoring models used for credit decisions, fraud detection, tenant screening, and marketing targeting.</p>

    <div class="broker-spotlight">
      <h3 class="spotlight-heading">Three of the Largest — There Are Thousands</h3>
      <p class="spotlight-intro">Most people encounter consumer-facing people-search sites directly. Below are three of the largest B2B data brokers — the ones powering advertising, insurance pricing, and identity resolution at scale.</p>
      <ul class="broker-links">
        <li>
          <a href="/data-brokers/acxiom" class="broker-link-item">
            <span class="broker-link-name">Acxiom</span>
            <span class="broker-link-desc">~2.5B consumer profiles globally. The partner data that powers ad targeting on platforms like Meta and Google often comes from Acxiom.</span>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/data-brokers/experian" class="broker-link-item">
            <span class="broker-link-name">Experian</span>
            <span class="broker-link-desc">The credit bureau also runs a 300M-record marketing database. Same company, separate division, separate opt-out.</span>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/data-brokers/liveramp" class="broker-link-item">
            <span class="broker-link-name">LiveRamp</span>
            <span class="broker-link-desc">Infrastructure, not data. LiveRamp connects everyone else's datasets — even if you opt out of every individual platform, LiveRamp may still be matching your identity across them.</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- HOW TO OPT OUT -->
  <div slot="how-to-turn-off">
    <WarningCallout title="Opt-outs are not permanent">
      <p>Data broker opt-outs are processed individually and do not persist indefinitely. As new public records are filed and new data is purchased from other sources, brokers may re-create suppressed profiles. Services like DeleteMe and Kanary automate recurring opt-out submissions to counteract this, but they cannot guarantee full suppression across all brokers. They do not cover B2B brokers that do not offer public-facing opt-outs.</p>
    </WarningCallout>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Doing It Yourself: People-Search Brokers</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Search your name on Spokeo, BeenVerified, WhitePages, Intelius, and Radaris. Note the URLs of pages containing your profile.</li>
        <li>Each broker has its own opt-out form, typically found by searching "[broker name] opt out." Submit your name, address, and the URL of the specific listing you want removed.</li>
        <li>Most opt-outs require email confirmation. Some require a government ID to verify identity — consider the tradeoff before submitting an ID to a data broker.</li>
        <li>Repeat this process every 6 to 12 months, as profiles frequently reappear after suppression.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Removal Services (DeleteMe, Kanary)</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>DeleteMe and Kanary automate opt-out submissions to hundreds of brokers on a recurring basis. They cost roughly $100 to $200 per year per person.</li>
        <li>They cover most consumer-facing people-search brokers. They do not have full coverage of B2B data brokers like Acxiom and LexisNexis, which do not offer public opt-out mechanisms equivalent to consumer-facing brokers.</li>
        <li>These services are useful for reducing exposure at people-search sites. They are not a complete solution for B2B broker data.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">CCPA Rights (California Residents)</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>California residents can submit data deletion requests to data brokers operating in California under CCPA. Brokers are legally required to respond within 45 days.</li>
        <li>California also maintains a Data Broker Registry at cppa.ca.gov listing registered brokers. This is not comprehensive but provides a starting list.</li>
        <li>As of 2024, California's Delete Act (SB 362) requires the state to establish a single opt-out mechanism for registered data brokers. This is being implemented through 2026.</li>
        <li>Other states with similar rights: Virginia, Colorado, Connecticut, Texas. Federal law does not provide equivalent rights outside of specific regulated categories (medical data, financial data, data on minors).</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Reduce Future Data Collection</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Opt out of loyalty programs where possible, or use a dedicated email address not linked to your main identity for programs you want to keep.</li>
        <li>Opt out of prescreened credit and insurance offers at optoutprescreen.com. This reduces one input into B2B marketing profiles.</li>
        <li>Use a P.O. box or mail forwarding service for accounts where your physical address is required but not for delivery. This limits address data proliferation.</li>
        <li>For public records: in many states, voter registration information can be marked as confidential if you qualify (domestic violence survivors, law enforcement, and in some states other grounds). Court records generally cannot be retroactively suppressed.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- WHY IT MATTERS -->
  <div slot="why-it-matters">
    <p>The relevant fact about data brokers is not that they have your address. Your address is in the phone book, more or less. The relevant fact is the combination: address history with financial behavior with health interest signals with behavioral scoring with relatives and associates, assembled into a single record sold to anyone willing to pay.</p>

    <p>B2B brokers like LexisNexis and Acxiom are deeply embedded in commercial infrastructure. LexisNexis risk products are used by insurance companies to price policies, by landlords to screen tenants, by employers for background checks, and by financial institutions for fraud detection. Acxiom's data reaches an estimated 2.5 billion consumer profiles globally and is used to build lookalike audience segments for advertising. These uses are largely invisible to the people whose data is being used.</p>

    <WarningCallout title="You cannot opt out of being a commercial product">
      <p>Opting out of data broker records removes or suppresses your profile from that broker's database. It does not stop other brokers from maintaining their own records. A profile removed from Spokeo is still available at BeenVerified. A profile suppressed at Acxiom is not affected by anything that happens at LexisNexis. Removal services that automate this process submit opt-outs to hundreds of brokers, but they cannot guarantee coverage of all brokers, and they cannot prevent new profiles from being created as new data flows in from public records and third-party sources.</p>
    </WarningCallout>

    <p>The practical consequence is that opt-out processes are individually useful but systemically limited. An opt-out at a people-search site reduces the likelihood that someone can find your home address with a basic web search. That is meaningful. It does not reduce your profile at the B2B brokers that power insurance pricing, tenant screening, and credit decisions.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- HOW IT WORKS -->
  <div slot="how-it-works">
    <ol class="steps-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Public records ingestion.</strong> Voter registration data, property records, court filings (civil suits, bankruptcies, criminal charges and convictions), professional licenses, marriage and divorce filings, and business registrations are public records in most US states. Data brokers systematically collect these records and incorporate them into profiles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Commercial data purchases.</strong> Retailers sell purchase history data, often through loyalty programs. When you swipe a grocery store loyalty card, that transaction data may be sold to data aggregators. Financial companies sell anonymized transaction records. These datasets are matched to individual profiles through name, address, and device identifiers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Social media scraping.</strong> Publicly visible social media profiles, posts, and activity are scraped and incorporated into profiles. Information you consider semi-private — workplace, relationships, interests — may be included if your social media settings were ever set to public.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Broker-to-broker data sales.</strong> Data brokers buy data from each other. A profile suppressed at one broker may have already been sold to several others before the opt-out was submitted. The same core record replicates across the ecosystem.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Profile inference and scoring.</strong> B2B brokers apply proprietary models to raw data to generate scored outputs: credit risk scores, fraud propensity scores, insurance risk scores, and consumer behavior segment assignments. These scores are often what gets sold, not the underlying records.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- WHO HAS YOUR DATA -->
  <div slot="who-has-data">
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Broker</th>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Primary Use</th>
            <th>Opt-Out Available</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Spokeo</td>
            <td>Consumer-facing (people search)</td>
            <td>Individual lookup; sold to consumers and investigators</td>
            <td>Yes, via spokeo.com/optout</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>BeenVerified</td>
            <td>Consumer-facing (people search)</td>
            <td>Individual lookup, background checks for consumers</td>
            <td>Yes, via beenverified.com/opt-out</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>WhitePages</td>
            <td>Consumer-facing (people search)</td>
            <td>Phone and address lookup</td>
            <td>Yes, via whitepages.com/suppression_requests</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Acxiom</td>
            <td>B2B marketing data</td>
            <td>Audience segments for advertisers; data enrichment</td>
            <td>Limited, via acxiom.com/opt-out-instruction</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>LexisNexis</td>
            <td>B2B risk and identity</td>
            <td>Insurance underwriting, tenant screening, fraud detection</td>
            <td>Limited; freeze available under some state laws</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Experian (marketing)</td>
            <td>B2B marketing data</td>
            <td>Audience targeting, credit pre-screening</td>
            <td>Opt-out from marketing products via optoutprescreen.com for prescreening</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- RELATED PRACTICES -->
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/third-party-sharing" label="Third-Party Data Sharing" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/location-data" label="Location Data" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/off-platform-tracking" label="Off-Platform Activity Tracking" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/cross-device" label="Cross-Device Graph Matching" type="practice" />

  <!-- RELATED PLATFORMS -->
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/google" label="Google" type="platform" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/meta" label="Meta / Instagram" type="platform" />

</PracticeLayout>

